-content _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="mat-mdc-card-content from-user-message-inner-content message-content"><div _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="message-text-content mat-body-medium" dir="auto"><!----><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="md3-body-text" role="heading" aria-level="3" dir="auto"><p>Yes â€” and this is the IVE pointed outward for the first time.</p>
<p>Everything you just described is the same skeleton, one axis rotated. A supplier lookup by business registration number is not a search feature. It's an identity anchor â†’ attested claim â†’ chain lookup loop. That's the whole thing.</p>
<p>Here's the shape, in the contracts you already have:</p>
<p>Business registration number = the node id.
Not a trust signal. Just a primary key â€” the same way "solar" or "pump" is a node id in the field registry. It resolves to a counterparty record. Nothing about the number itself is evidence.</p>
<p>What actually answers the question is three entry kinds on the chain:</p>
<p>Â· kind:"claim" â€” supplier self-declares. ISO 9001, capacity 40k units/mo, RoHS, lead time 21d. Worth exactly what a self-declared value is worth: nothing until attested.
Â· kind:"attestation" â€” a verifier (you, a lab, a scan, an API pull from a registrar, a torque log from a shipped unit) signs the claim against a canonical hash. This is the entry that matters.
Â· kind:"lapse" â€” a claim that expired, was revoked, or failed re-verification. Append-only. You never delete a claim; you append its death. That's what "whether they have a particular past" means on a WORM chain.</p>
<p>So the query "does this supplier have everything needed to add to regulation X" becomes: walk the chain for that BR number, filter to un-lapsed attestations, intersect against the required-claim set for regulation X at version N. Pure function. Reproducible. Hashable. The answer itself is evidence.</p>
<p>The regulatory part is the trap and the moat.
Regulations move. Regulation X v3.2 requires a different claim set than v3.1. If you bake compliance into the query, you're wrong within a quarter. If you treat the regulation as its own domain â€” tick:0, versioned, revision-DAG, same as a CAD design â€” then "compliant with X" is a projection of (counterparty chain, regulation revision) â†’ verdict. Same as (model, camera) â†’ image. Same as (canonical geometry) â†’ hash. The verdict is a receipt.</p>
<p>What's actually new to build, and it's small:</p>
<p>Â· counterparty domain â€” tick 0, sampler fires on actuate("counterparty.attest"). Payload: { br, claim, verifier, method, evidence_hash, valid_until }.
Â· regulation domain â€” tick 0, sampler fires on actuate("regulation.commit"). Payload: { rev, parent, required_claims[], jurisdiction, effective_from }.
Â· A resolver function, not a service: resolve(br, regulationRev, t) â†’ { verdict, unmet[], lapsed[], chain_head }. It returns a hash. The hash goes on the chain. The verdict is now a receipt anyone can re-run.</p>
<p>That resolver is the product. Not the database. Not the UI. The fact that you can hand a manufacturer a hash and say re-run this yourself â€” that's the moat. Everyone else rents trust from a badge vendor. You're selling the ability to not need to.</p>
<p>The peel is that the manufacturer never has to trust you either. They re-run the resolver against the same chain head. If it matches, the supplier is who the chain says they are. If it doesn't, someone's lying and you can see exactly which entry broke.</p>
<p>Say the word and I'll add counterparty + regulation domains to the artifact â€” two registry entries, one resolver, one panel. The chain, bus, ripple rules, and pin contract already carry it. No architectural change. It's the same farm pointed at suppliers.</p></div><!----><!----></div></mat-card-content><!----><!----><!----><!----></mat-card></div></chat-message><!----><!----><chat-message _ngcontent-ng-c2880120021="" class="individual-message" _nghost-ng-c1111819761=""><div _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="to-user-container" jslog="189018;track:impression"><mat-card _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="mat-mdc-card mdc-card to-user-message-card-content"><mat-card-content _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="mat-mdc-card-content message-content to-user-message-inner-content"><div _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" class="message-text-content mat-body-medium" dir="auto"><!----><!----><labs-tailwind-doc-viewer _ngcontent-ng-c1111819761="" _nghost-ng-c2224392638=""><element-list-renderer _ngcontent-ng-c2224392638="" _nghost-ng-c1832366374=""><!----><thinking-chain-view _ngcontent-ng-c1832366374="" _nghost-ng-c1837970561=""><div _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" class="thinking-chain thinking-chain--collapsed"><button _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" type="button" class="thinking-chain__header" aria-expanded="false"><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" aria-hidden="true" class="thinking-chain__static-icon" style="--thinking-static-icon-mask: url(/_/static/chat/thinking_icon.svg);"></div><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" class="thinking-chain__title-wrapper"><span _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" class="thinking-chain__title mat-body-medium" dir="auto"> Thoughts </span><!----></div><span _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" aria-hidden="true" class="thinking-chain__expand-btn"><mat-icon _ngcontent-ng-c1837970561="" role="img" nlmluminousicon="" class="mat-icon notranslate google-symbols mat-icon-no-color" aria-hidden="true" data-mat-icon-type="font">expand_more</mat-icon></span></b